Lesson 23: Modules — import aur export in TypeScript 🚀
Ab tak humne code mostly ek hi file mein likha. Real projects mein code ko alag-alag files mein rakhte hain, taaki project maintain karna easy ho.
Maan lo tum ek application bana rahe ho. Ek file mein user ka logic, doosri mein calculation aur teesri mein main application ka code hoga.
1. export kya karta hai?
math.ts naam ki file banao:
export function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}

export const PI = 3.14;


Yahan export ka matlab hai ki doosri files is function aur constant ko use kar sakti hain.
2. import kya karta hai?
Ab main.ts file banao:
import { add, PI } from "./math";

console.log(add(10, 20));
console.log(PI);


Output:
30
3.14


math.ts
Function aur constant export karta hai.


import

main.ts
Export kiye gaye items ko use karta hai.



3. Default export
Agar ek file se ek main item export karna ho, toh default export use kar sakte hain.
user.ts:
export default class User {
  constructor(public name: string) {}

  greet(): string {
    return `Hello, ${this.name}`;
  }
}


main.ts:
import User from "./user";

const user = new User("Rahul");
console.log(user.greet());


Output:
Hello, Rahul


Difference yaad rakho:
- Named export: import { add } from "./math" — curly braces use hote hain.
- Default export: import User from "./user" — aam taur par curly braces nahi lagte.
4. Sirf types export karna
TypeScript mein types ko bhi alag files mein export kar sakte ho.
types.ts:
export type Product = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  price: number;
};


main.ts:
import type { Product } from "./types";

const item: Product = {
  id: 1,
  name: "Laptop",
  price: 50000
};

console.log(item.name);


import type batata hai ki humein sirf type information chahiye, runtime JavaScript value nahi.
Note: Project ke tsconfig.json aur module configuration ke hisaab se import paths mein .js extension ya doosre rules ki zarurat ho sakti hai.
